{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>צְנוּמוֹת דַּקּוֹת (withered and thin).</b> צוּנְמָא in Aramaic means a rock (סֶלַע) — so they were like dry wood without moisture and hard as rock. And the Targum renders it נָצַן לָקְיָן; נָצַן — they had nothing in them but the new growth (נֵץ), because they were emptied of grain. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 41:23). But ears of grain, at the time they are emptied of seed, have no new growth in them. And similarly Pharaoh did not see them at the time of sprouting, for it was when they were full and good and when they were thin and blasted by the east wind that he saw them. And likewise, to say that צְנוּמוֹת means like rocks (סְלוּעוֹת), from the word צוּנְמָא, is not correct, for thin ears are not hard as rock. Rather, the meaning of צְנוּמוֹת is: broken into many pieces. This is the language of our Rabbis (Berakhot 39a): פַּת הַצְּנוּמָה בַּקְּעָרָה — one recites הַמּוֹצִיא over it; it is cut-up bread — it is the loaf that a person cuts into pieces and pours broth over it in a bowl, eating it piece by piece without another act of breaking bread. And this is likewise the language of the Targum, which says נָצַן — meaning cutting and deficiency, as they, of blessed memory, said (Bava Batra 54a): מֻלְיָא בְּמֻלְיָא נַצָּא בְּנַצָּא לָא הָוְיָא חֲזָקָה, שְׁקַל מֻלְיָא וּשְׁדָא בְּנַצָּא הָוְיָא חֲזָקָה — a place that is elevated, where the earth is whole, is called מַלְיָא (full), and one where the earth is missing, where there is a deficiency, is called נַצָּא. So too here, צְנוּמוֹת is the opposite of מְלֵאוֹת (full), and נָצַן is the opposite of מְלֵאָן (full). The meaning is that the ears were not full of grains, but had empty places in them with no grains, while in another place they were elevated where the grains had withered, and they were hollow. Therefore Joseph said, in place of צְנוּמוֹת, רַקּוֹת (Genesis 41:27), for they had empty places in them with no grains at all."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi's explanation that צוּנְמָא means סֶלַע (rock) in Aramaic, then disputes it, arguing instead that צְנוּמוֹת means broken into pieces, citing Berakhot 39a and Bava Batra 54a. The Aramaic phrases from Bava Batra are left untranslated as they are technical legal terms about land measurement."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:43:40.941649400Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}